answer.en:
Choose WebSocket when both sides need a long-lived, bidirectional session, such as interactive collaboration or chat. Choose SSE when the server is the only sender and the browser needs a stream of updates over HTTP; `EventSource` also has built-in reconnection behavior. SSE uses `text/event-stream`, UTF-8 messages separated by blank lines, and can carry event names, data, IDs, and a retry interval. A WebSocket client must handle close and error events, reconnect deliberately, authenticate the new connection, and define message ordering and replay expectations. For either protocol I would bound resources, send keep-alives where needed, and close connections when the page no longer needs them.

```ts caption="EventSource reconnects by default; close it when the page no longer needs the stream."
const stream = new EventSource('/events');
stream.onmessage = (event) => {
  const update = JSON.parse(event.data);
  render(update);
};
stream.onerror = (error) => {
  console.warn('SSE connection error', error);
};
window.addEventListener('pagehide', () => stream.close());
```
